          "label": "<p>A patient with recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones and mild hypertension nee</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones and mild hypertension needs a diuretic. Which of the following is the MOST appropriate choice?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Furosemide</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Loop diuretics like furosemide cause urinary calcium loss by blocking NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb. This would worsen calcium stone risk, not reduce it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hydrochlorothiazide</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Thiazide diuretics promote calcium retention at the distal convoluted tubule, reducing urinary calcium excretion. This treats hypertension while also lowering the risk of recurrent calcium stones.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Spironolactone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Potassium-sparing diuretics act on the collecting duct and do not offer the calcium-retaining benefit needed for stone prevention. They are mainly used for their anti-aldosterone effects.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acetazolamide</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This carbonic anhydrase inhibitor causes weak diuresis and metabolic acidosis. It is used for glaucoma and altitude sickness, not for hypertension or calcium stone prevention.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key distinction is calcium handling: loop diuretics waste calcium (risking hypocalcaemia), while thiazides retain it (useful in calcium stones but contraindicated in hypercalcaemia). The nephron site of action determines this difference."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Thiazides promote renal calcium retention — a unique property among diuretics that makes them the drug of choice when hypertension coexists with calcium stone disease."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
