          "label": "<p>A 65-year-old patient with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is on en</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 65-year-old patient with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is on enalapril and furosemide. The physician plans to add a beta-blocker. Which of the following has proven mortality-reduction evidence in HFrEF?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Atenolol 50 mg once daily</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Atenolol has no mortality-reduction data in heart failure and is not recommended for this indication. It is an older beta-1 selective blocker used primarily for hypertension and angina.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Metoprolol tartrate 50 mg twice daily</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The immediate-release formulation (tartrate) produces unpredictable blood levels and lacks heart failure mortality data. Only the extended-release form (metoprolol succinate) is evidence-based for HFrEF.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bisoprolol 1.25 mg once daily, titrated upward</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Only three beta-blockers have proven mortality benefit in HFrEF: carvedilol, bisoprolol, and metoprolol succinate. Treatment is started at a low dose and uptitrated gradually.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Propranolol 40 mg three times daily</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Propranolol is a non-selective beta-blocker without mortality-reduction evidence in heart failure. Its short duration and non-selectivity make it unsuitable for HFrEF.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Not all beta-blockers are equivalent in heart failure. Only carvedilol, bisoprolol, and metoprolol succinate have mortality-reduction evidence. Atenolol, metoprolol tartrate, and propranolol are not interchangeable for this indication."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Bisoprolol is one of only three beta-blockers with proven mortality benefit in HFrEF, alongside carvedilol and metoprolol succinate."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
