          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome develops atrial fibrillation with </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome develops atrial fibrillation with a rapid ventricular response. Which of the following drugs is SAFE to administer?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intravenous verapamil</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Verapamil blocks the AV node but not the accessory pathway. With the AV route blocked, fibrillatory impulses conduct exclusively through the accessory pathway at rates up to 350 bpm, risking ventricular fibrillation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intravenous digoxin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Digoxin slows the AV node via vagal effects but does not affect the accessory pathway. Like verapamil, it leaves the bypass tract wide open and can precipitate ventricular fibrillation in WPW.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intravenous adenosine</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adenosine transiently blocks AV node conduction but has no effect on the accessory pathway. Even its brief action can allow a dangerous surge of impulses through the bypass tract.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intravenous amiodarone</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Amiodarone prolongs the refractory period in both the AV node and the accessory pathway, making it safe in WPW with atrial fibrillation. Procainamide and DC cardioversion are also appropriate alternatives.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In WPW with atrial fibrillation, any drug that blocks only the AV node (verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin, adenosine) is contraindicated. These leave the accessory pathway open, allowing fibrillatory impulses to reach the ventricles at 250–350 bpm. Safe choices are amiodarone, procainamide, or DC cardioversion."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Amiodarone is safe because it blocks conduction through both the AV node and the accessory pathway, preventing the dangerous unopposed bypass-tract conduction that the other three drugs cause."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
